From: Daniel Golle <daniel@makrotopia.org>
From: Frank Wunderlich <frank-w@public-files.de>
Subject: [PATCH] net: ethernet: mtk_eth_soc: fix named-IRQ lookup fallback

Combines two upstream mainline fixes to mtk_get_irqs(), both already in
Linux stable as of 6.12.105/6.12.106:

1. "net: ethernet: mtk_eth_soc: only use legacy mode on missing IRQ
   name" (070e98dd4e26279280e15db8f37077a001ca1b4c upstream). If
   platform_get_irq_byname() fails with anything other than -ENXIO
   ("name not present in this DT"), the driver must propagate that
   error instead of silently falling through to legacy index-based
   IRQ lookup.

2. "net: ethernet: mtk_eth_soc: improve support for named interrupts"
   (e81d36d48880ab3f2b351ce3df799acaa8b11c4f upstream). Switches to
   platform_get_irq_byname_optional(), which does not log its own
   error when the name is simply absent (the expected case for a
   legacy, index-based DT), and adds one clear driver-side dev_warn
   instead.

Applicability (local, 2026-09-27): this board's DTS uses legacy
index-based ethernet interrupts, not named ones, so eth->irq[MTK_FE_IRQ_TX/RX]
always come back -ENXIO here and the driver falls through to the
legacy per-index loop below (unchanged by this patch - that loop is a
separate refactor from a later upstream commit, not ported here to keep
this change minimal). Confirmed live: this router's dmesg has always
carried
  mtk_soc_eth 1b100000.ethernet: error -ENXIO: IRQ fe1 not found
  mtk_soc_eth 1b100000.ethernet: error -ENXIO: IRQ fe2 not found
at every boot - platform_get_irq_byname()'s own unconditional dev_err
for the "name absent" case, benign (the legacy fallback always
succeeds) but noisy. This replaces it with one clear dev_warn.

Signed-off-by: pat <pi3iman@gmail.com>
---
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/mediatek/mtk_eth_soc.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/mediatek/mtk_eth_soc.c
@@ -4097,11 +4097,23 @@
 	int i;
 
 	/* future SoCs beginning with MT7988 should use named IRQs in dts */
-	eth->irq[MTK_FE_IRQ_TX] = platform_get_irq_byname(pdev, "fe1");
-	eth->irq[MTK_FE_IRQ_RX] = platform_get_irq_byname(pdev, "fe2");
+	eth->irq[MTK_FE_IRQ_TX] = platform_get_irq_byname_optional(pdev, "fe1");
+	eth->irq[MTK_FE_IRQ_RX] = platform_get_irq_byname_optional(pdev, "fe2");
 	if (eth->irq[MTK_FE_IRQ_TX] >= 0 && eth->irq[MTK_FE_IRQ_RX] >= 0)
 		return 0;
 
+	/* only use legacy mode if platform_get_irq_byname_optional returned -ENXIO */
+	if (eth->irq[MTK_FE_IRQ_TX] != -ENXIO)
+		return dev_err_probe(&pdev->dev, eth->irq[MTK_FE_IRQ_TX],
+				     "Error requesting FE TX IRQ\n");
+
+	if (eth->irq[MTK_FE_IRQ_RX] != -ENXIO)
+		return dev_err_probe(&pdev->dev, eth->irq[MTK_FE_IRQ_RX],
+				     "Error requesting FE RX IRQ\n");
+
+	if (!MTK_HAS_CAPS(eth->soc->caps, MTK_SHARED_INT))
+		dev_warn(&pdev->dev, "legacy DT: missing interrupt-names.");
+
 	/* legacy way:
 	 * On MTK_SHARED_INT SoCs (MT7621 + MT7628) the first IRQ is taken
 	 * from devicetree and used for both RX and TX - it is shared.
